      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2014 The Obesity Society.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "Examine whether there are independent influences of a greater degree of adiposity and longer duration of obesity on cardiac structure and function."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Participants of CARDIA were 18-30 years when they underwent a baseline examination in 1985-86. Seven follow-up examinations were conducted every 2-5 years."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Among 2,547 participants who underwent an echocardiogram at the year 25 examination and were not obese at baseline, 34.4 and 35.5% were overall (BMI\u2009≥\u200930 kg m(-2) ) and abdominally obese (waist circumference: men: >102 cm; women: >88 cm) at year 25, respectively. A greater degree of overall and abdominal adiposity at year 25 were each associated with a greater left ventricular (LV) mass (P\u2009<\u20090.001), LV volume (P\u2009<\u20090.001), LV mass-to-volume ratio (P\u2009<\u20090.001), left atrial dimension (P\u2009<\u20090.001), and ejection fraction (P\u2009<\u20090.05) after adjustment for duration of obesity and other risk factors. In contrast, a longer duration of overall obesity was associated with a greater LV mass (P\u2009=\u20090.003) and a trend for a lower ejection fraction (P\u2009=\u20090.07)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "A greater degree of adiposity is strongly associated with concentric LV remodeling in midlife, while the cumulative effects of a longer duration of overall obesity during young adulthood contribute to concentric remodeling predominantly by increasing LV mass."
          }
        ]
      },
